>> title: Wu Gok
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: taro, pork, chinese, deep-fry, dumpling
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: woo gok, wu kok, 炸芋角, taro dumpling, taro puff, deep fried taro pork puff
>> servings: 12
>> time: 2 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the oil is 320°F and no hotter, because the honeycomb forms as the fat renders slowly out and a hot pot just browns it

>> step: steam the taro 25 min, mash it hot
Steam @taro{1%lb}(450 g; peeled and cut in 1-in slices) in a #steamer{} for ~steam{25%min}, until a skewer goes through without meeting anything, then mash it while it is still steaming dry.

>> step: beat the wheat starch paste in, chill 1 hr
Beat @wheat starch{1/2%cup}(60 g; scalded to a paste with 1/3 cup boiling water), @lard{3%Tbs}, @sugar{2%tsp}, @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} and @Chinese five-spice powder{1/4%tsp} into @&(~1)mashed taro{} while it is hot, then ~chill{1%hr} until it firms up enough to hold a shape.

>> step: fry the pork 5 min, spread it out, cool 20 min
Fry @ground pork{6%oz}(170 g) with @dried shiitake mushrooms{2}(soaked and diced), @shallot{1}(minced), @oyster sauce{1%Tbs}, @light soy sauce{2%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, @white pepper{1/4%tsp} and @cornstarch{1%tsp}(slaked in 1 Tbs water) in a #wok{} for ~fry{5%min}, then spread it on a tray and ~cool{20%min}.

>> step: wrap the filling in the taro dough, 12 ovals
Divide @&(~2)taro dough{} into 12, flatten each piece in an oiled palm, close @&(~1)filling{} inside it and roll it into an oval.

>> step: fry 320°F (160°C) 4 min, until the lace stands out
Fry @&(~1)ovals{} three at a time in @peanut oil{6%cups}(1.4 L; at 320°F/160°C) for ~fry{4%min}, leaving them alone, until a honeycomb of lace stands out all round.
